"A Western listener usually arrives with one comparison already made. Buddhism is like Stoicism, or like therapy, or like the mysticism of his own church. Do not begin by correcting it. The comparison is the listeners raft, and it has carried him this far. Begin by agreeing with what is true in it, and name the thinker if you can, since a familiar name earns trust. Then say plainly where the likeness ends, and give the reason in terms of purpose. The pattern has three parts: Yes, like X in this respect. Unlike X in that one. The difference matters because of what the teaching is for. People remember the second part best when the first has been granted.": "97840f02fcdb",
"A group of recent philosophers trained in both Sanskrit or Tibetan sources and analytic philosophy have proposed that the traditions be treated as partners on a problem. Siderits calls it fusion philosophy: use Buddhist arguments to make progress on a question such as personal identity, and use Western tools to test the Buddhist arguments. Garfield asks Western philosophers to engage Buddhist texts as they would engage Hume, as sources of positions that may be true. This is the approach closest to the one used on this shelf. Its risk is the mirror in a subtler form. What gets taken up is what present-day philosophy can use, and rebirth, meditation, and the monastic life are set aside as background. The Buddhist would say that the arguments were made for the sake of that background.": "7e9b999e6f17",
"A second passage concerns what happens when a teaching is taken hold of in the wrong way. A monk named Ariṭṭha had concluded that the things the Buddha called obstacles were no obstacle to him. The Buddha compared him to a man who needs a snake and seizes it by the coils or the tail. It turns and bites him. A man who pins it behind the head with a forked stick can hold it safely. In the same way, teachings wrongly grasped lead to harm and suffering. In the same discourse he gives the simile of the raft. A traveler builds a raft to cross a flood, and on the far shore he does not carry it on his head out of gratitude. The teaching is for crossing over, not for holding on. The two images belong together. A teaching has a use, and it can be picked up by the wrong end.": "2eafc4f65f12",
"A third passage gives the test by which he sorted what to teach. In a grove near Kosambī he picked up a few leaves and asked the monks which were more, the leaves in his hand or those overhead. What he knew and had not taught, he said, was like the leaves of the forest. He had left it out because its not beneficial or relevant to the fundamentals of the spiritual life. What he taught was suffering, its origin, its cessation, and the path. Here is a principle for comparison stated by the Buddha himself. To understand a teaching, ask what it is for. Two doctrines that sound alike may serve different ends, and two that sound opposed may be doing the same work.": "c3cfcdf7a364",
"Buddhism has been through this once already, and the record is instructive. When the sutras reached China, educated readers knew the Laozi, the Zhuangzi, and the Book of Changes, and nothing of India. In the fourth century a monk named Zhu Faya, whose students were well-versed in secular works, but did not yet excel in Buddhist principles, took the numbered lists of the sutras and correlated them with passages from Chinese books as aids to explanation. This was called geyi, matching concepts. The practice in that narrow sense was short-lived. The wider habit it stands for lasted longer. Early translators rendered nirvana by the Daoist term for non-action and suchness by a term meaning original non-being, and for a century Chinese thinkers debated emptiness as though it were the nothingness from which, in Laozi, all things come. The great translator Kumārajīva, arriving in 401, replaced much of this vocabulary, and his student Sengzhao showed case by case that emptiness in Nāgārjunas sense is neither being nor non-being.": "12dcd2a539e7",
"Each lesson on this shelf names the Western thinker closest to a Buddhist teaching and says where the likeness ends. The judgment is reached by asking three questions in order. Do they reach the same conclusion? Do they reach it by the same argument? Do they hold it for the same purpose? Take the pairing of the first lesson after this one. Derek Parfit and the Buddha reach the same conclusion about persons: there is no further fact, beyond a stream of connected events, that makes me me. Their arguments overlap in part. Parfit works from imagined cases of division and replication, and the Buddha from the observation that nothing in experience is permanent or under control. Their purposes differ. Parfit offers a true theory and reports that it made him less afraid of death. The Buddha offers a practice whose aim is the end of clinging. A sentence that says all three things is a comparison. Parfit was a Buddhist is a slogan.": "4b56aac5063f",
"Hume looked for a self and found only passing perceptions. The Buddha said the same, and then asked what follows for how we live.": "22036a79e5ea",
"I would not say they teach the same thing. I would say they noticed the same problem.": "9eec320e641f",
"In The Perennial Philosophy Huxley gathered passages from Christian mystics, Sufis, Vedāntins, Daoists, and Buddhists to show that all of them, at their highest, teach one doctrine: a divine Ground of all being, with which the human spirit is identical and which it can come to know directly. The book is generous and made many readers curious about traditions they had dismissed. Its method is to select what fits and to treat the rest as local dress. Buddhism fits poorly. The Buddha taught that no self is to be found, and the early discourses decline to affirm a Ground of being. To make Buddhism say the perennial thing, the not-self teaching has to be read as pointing to a higher Self, which is the reading the Buddha rejected. The blender delivers agreement by removing what each tradition took most trouble to say.": "227a31a81f48",
"Schopenhauer was the first major European philosopher to take Indian thought seriously. He read the Upanishads in a Latin version made from a Persian one, and called them the solace of his life. Of Buddhism he wrote that if he took his own results as the standard of truth, he would have to rank it above all other religions, and he was pleased to find his teaching in such agreement with it. The sentence shows the method. His own system is the measure, and Buddhism is praised for resembling it. He saw correctly that the Buddha starts from suffering and traces it to craving. He then read nirvana as the denial of the will to live and Buddhism as a pessimism like his own, and missed that the third noble truth is good news. A mirror gives real information about one side of the room.": "9e4262752b17",
"That is a real likeness, and it holds at the level of the conclusion. The reasons are different.": "38680bc76c9c",
"The Buddha said his teaching was a raft: for crossing over, not for holding on. So the first question about any Buddhist idea is what it is for.": "9e4f377e2f20",
"The Buddha taught in a crowded field. The discourses name materialists, fatalists, skeptics, ascetics of several kinds, and brahmins of the old religion, and wanderers came to him regularly to ask how his teaching differed from the others. His best-known comment on such debates is a story. A king has an elephant shown to people blind from birth. Each is led to one part. Asked what an elephant is like, those who touched the head say a pot, those who touched the ear a winnowing fan, the tusk a plowshare, the leg a pillar, the tail a pestle, and they come to blows. The Buddhas verse on the scene ends: Arguing, they dispute, the folk who see just one part. The story is often told to show that all views are equally true. In the text it shows that a partial view defended as the whole is a cause of quarrels. The king who can see the animal is not in the same position as the men who cannot.": "e68956c3a46b",
"The Stoics and the Buddha agree that our suffering comes largely from how we respond to events. They part on what to do about it.": "337285dd36fe",
"The third question is given the last word because the Buddha gave it the last word. The handful of leaves makes purpose the test of what belongs in the teaching. Where a Western view matches a Buddhist one in conclusion and argument and differs in purpose, the lesson will call it the nearest view and will say that it stops short. That is a description of where each stands. It does not mean that the Western view is worse philosophy. A reader who thinks the purpose mistaken is free to prefer the view that stops short.": "d419928f7c6f",
"Two lessons can be drawn. Matching was a necessary first step, since no one can learn a new thing except by relating it to what he knows, and the Buddha himself borrowed and redefined the brahmins words. And matching has to be outgrown. Once it had the texts in better translation, the Chinese tradition developed its own tool for comparison, the classification of teachings, which ranks doctrines by the audience and purpose each was meant for. That is the test of the handful of leaves turned into a method. The step the Dharma takes beyond any theory of comparison is to treat views themselves as things that can be clung to. A comparison made to win, or to prove that ones own tradition had everything first, is the snake taken by the tail. Made to understand what someone else was trying to cross, it is part of the path.": "e9c988f4f9c4",
"When Buddhism first came to China, people read it as a kind of Daoism. It took about two hundred years to see the difference, so there is no hurry.": "d0917276ac53"
}
